{%- comment -%}
  Render a Shopify product description as the same bordered card grid the
  bottles use for custom.benefits.

  WHY THIS EXISTS. The sets carry their copy in Shopify's description field,
  written years ago as CONTAINS / ABOUT / PROFILE with lists beneath each. When
  the page finally printed it, it printed it as what it is — a wall of headings
  and bullets, which is the one thing on the site that looks like a different
  website. The Stopper's page next to it reads as designed, because its copy
  lives in custom.benefits and gets the card grid.

  Rather than ask someone to retype six descriptions into a metafield, this
  reads the structure that is already in the HTML: every heading becomes a card
  title, everything under it becomes that card's body. The copy stays where it
  is, editable where it has always been editable, and the page stops looking
  like two different pages.

  HOW THE PARSE WORKS. Liquid has no HTML parser, so the heading levels are
  normalised to a single sentinel and the string is split on it. Nested markup
  inside a heading is fine and expected — Shopify's own editor writes
  `<h2 dir="ltr"><span>CONTAINS</span></h2>` — because the title is recovered
  by rebuilding the element and running strip_html over the whole thing, not
  by slicing the string at a bracket. See the note beside that code for the
  two ways slicing got it wrong first.

  Anything before the first heading is kept as an untitled lead card rather
  than dropped, because silently discarding copy is how a page loses a
  paragraph nobody notices for a year.

  Accepts: html (required), label (optional eyebrow).
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign src = html

  # Normalise every heading level to one sentinel so a single split finds them
  # all. Done open and close separately: the open tag may carry attributes.
  assign src = src | replace: '<h1', '<hX' | replace: '<h2', '<hX' | replace: '<h3', '<hX' | replace: '<h4', '<hX' | replace: '<h5', '<hX' | replace: '<h6', '<hX'
  assign src = src | replace: '</h1>', '</hX>' | replace: '</h2>', '</hX>' | replace: '</h3>', '</hX>' | replace: '</h4>', '</hX>' | replace: '</h5>', '</hX>' | replace: '</h6>', '</hX>'

  assign chunks = src | split: '<hX'
-%}

{%- capture cards -%}
  {%- for chunk in chunks -%}
    {%- if chunk contains '</hX>' -%}
      {%- assign parts = chunk | split: '</hX>' -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        PUT THE OPENING BRACKET BACK, THEN STRIP.

        parts[0] is the heading's insides plus its own attributes, missing only
        the `<` that the split consumed: ` dir="ltr"><span>CONTAINS</span>`.
        Prepending `<hX` makes that a well-formed element again and strip_html
        removes all of it cleanly.

        The first attempt took `split: '>' | last` instead, which cut the
        string mid-tag and produced `CONTAINS</span`. strip_html only removes
        COMPLETE tags, so the unterminated `</span` survived — and an
        unterminated tag inside the title div makes the browser parser swallow
        the following siblings, so every card's heading contained its own body.

        The second attempt fixed that and appended a `>` as well, which
        parts[0] already ends with, so every title rendered as `CONTAINS>`.
        Nothing to append: the closing bracket was never eaten.

        This also retires the note further up about headings holding no nested
        markup. Shopify's editor writes `<h2 dir="ltr"><span>X</span></h2>`, so
        that was false on every set the moment it was written.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- capture heading -%}<hX{{ parts[0] }}{%- endcapture -%}
      {%- assign title = heading | strip_html | strip -%}
      {%- assign body = parts[1] | strip -%}
    {%- else -%}
      {%- comment -%} Copy sitting above the first heading. Kept, untitled. {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- assign title = '' -%}
      {%- assign body = chunk | strip -%}
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- assign plain = body | strip_html | strip -%}
    {%- if title != blank or plain != blank -%}
      <div class="non-benefit">
        {%- if title != blank -%}
          <div class="non-benefit__t">{{ title }}</div>
        {%- endif -%}
        {%- if plain != blank -%}
          <div class="non-benefit__b non-benefit__b--rich">{{ body }}</div>
        {%- endif -%}
      </div>
    {%- endif -%}
  {%- endfor -%}
{%- endcapture -%}

{%- comment -%}
  An empty grid is worse than no grid — it renders as a stray border. Counting
  the cards that actually came out is the only honest test, because a
  description of pure whitespace still splits into chunks.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- assign card_count = cards | split: 'class="non-benefit"' | size | minus: 1 -%}
{%- if card_count > 0 -%}
  <div class="non-benefits">
    {%- if label != blank -%}
      <div class="non-mono non-eyebrow">{{ label }}</div>
    {%- endif -%}
    <div class="non-benefits__list">{{ cards }}</div>
  </div>
{%- endif -%}
